## Solution

**Answer:** $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{E(n)}{n} = \frac{1-e^{-2}}{2}.$

### Setup and drift

Starting from $a_{n,0}=1$, at each step $m_{n,k}$ is uniform on $\{1,\dots,n\}$ and
$$a_{n,k+1}=a_{n,k}+\begin{cases}+1 & m>a\\ 0 & m=a\\ -1 & m<a.\end{cases}$$
The state stays in $\{1,\dots,n\}$: at $a=1$ only $+1$ or $0$ is possible; at $a=n$ only $-1$ or $0$.

Given $a_{n,k}=a$, the conditional drift is
$$\mu_n(a):=\mathbb{E}[\Delta a\mid a]=\frac{n-a}{n}-\frac{a-1}{n}=\frac{n+1-2a}{n},$$
and the conditional variance is $\sigma_n^2(a)=\frac{n-1}{n}-\mu_n(a)^2\le 1$.

### Martingale decomposition

Set $x_k:=a_{n,k}/n$ and define the martingale
$$M_k:=a_{n,k}-a_{n,0}-\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\mu_n(a_{n,j}),\qquad M_0=0.$$
Its increments are bounded by $1$ in absolute value, so $\mathbb{E}[M_n^2]=\sum_{j}\mathbb{E}[\operatorname{Var}(\Delta a\mid a_{n,j})]\le n$. Dividing by $n$,
$$\frac{M_n}{n}\to 0 \quad\text{in }L^2,\text{ hence in probability.}\tag{1}$$

### Euler scheme identification

From the decomposition,
$$x_{k+1}-x_k=\frac{1}{n}\Bigl(1+\tfrac1n-2x_k\Bigr)+\frac{M_{k+1}-M_k}{n}.$$
Writing $h:=1/n$ and $\xi_k:=(M_{k+1}-M_k)/n$, this is
$$x_{k+1}=x_k+h(1-2x_k)+h^2+\xi_k,\qquad x_0=h.\tag{2}$$
The $\xi_k$ are martingale differences with $|\xi_k|\le 1/n$ and total variance $\sum_k\mathbb{E}[\xi_k^2]\le n\cdot n^{-2}=1/n\to 0$, so by Doob/Chebyshev
$$\max_{k\le n}\Bigl|\sum_{j<k}\xi_j\Bigr|\to 0\quad\text{in probability.}\tag{3}$$

Equation (2) is the explicit (forward) Euler discretization of the ODE
$$\dot x = 1-2x,\qquad x(0)=0,\tag{4}$$
with step $h=1/n$, up to two perturbations: a deterministic drift of size $h^2$ per step (cumulative $O(h)$) and the vanishing martingale noise (3).

### Convergence to the ODE solution

The ODE (4) is globally Lipschitz, so Euler's method has global error $O(h)$. Comparing the Euler iterates $\tilde x_k$ (without perturbations, $\tilde x_0=0$) to the exact solution $x(t)=\frac12(1-e^{-2t})$ at $t_k=kh$ gives $\max_{k\le n}|\tilde x_k-x(t_k)|\le Ch$.

The perturbations in (2) contribute at most $O(h)$ from the $h^2$ terms and $o(1)$ from (3). Therefore
$$x_n \;\xrightarrow{p}\; x(1)=\tfrac12(1-e^{-2}).\tag{5}$$

### From probability to expectation

Since $a_{n,k}\in\{1,\dots,n\}$, we have $x_n\in[0,1]$, a bounded family. Convergence in probability of bounded random variables implies convergence of expectations:
$$\frac{E(n)}{n}=\mathbb{E}[x_n]\;\longrightarrow\;\frac12(1-e^{-2}).$$

